// Regression test: List<int>.push() must reject an argument of the wrong
// type at COMPILE time. Before this fix, struct method calls (unlike free
// function / module calls) never checked argument TYPES at all -- only
// argument COUNT -- because `push(item: T)`'s declared parameter type is
// the generic placeholder T, which trivially "matched" anything with no
// check. Confirmed as a genuine type-safety hole, not just a missing
// diagnostic: `l.push("hello")` on a `List<int>` compiled clean, and the
// next `l.pop()` read the string's raw pointer bits back reinterpreted as
// an int. See TypeChecker::visitMethodCall()'s TypeMatches filter (mirrors
// the equivalent check the module-call path already had) for the fix.

import list::List;

fn main(): int {
    mut l: List<int> = List::default();
    l.push("hello");
    return 0;
}
